Administrator Guide: Blocking Microsoft Mail, Outlook, and OneDrive via Command Prompt
Overview & Scope
This guide outlines administrative procedures to disable or block Microsoft Mail, Outlook, and Microsoft OneDrive on Windows 10 and Windows 11 workstations using the Windows Command Prompt (cmd.exe). These methods are specifically designed for standalone, non-domain, and small-office environments where administrators need to restrict end-user access to built-in communication and cloud synchronization tools without removing core system packages or requiring complex Active Directory Group Policy infrastructures. Always test this out on one device or a virtual computer first before setting up on other computers.
Blocking Microsoft Mail and Outlook (Without Uninstalling)
When administrators need to retain application files on disk while completely preventing users from launching or configuring email accounts, execution restriction policies are the recommended approach.
Method A: System-Wide Execution Redirection (IFEO) 
Image File Execution Options (IFEO) redirect binary execution to a benign process (such as systray.exe), causing the application to close instantly without launching its GUI or background sync engines. To apply this configuration, launch Command Prompt as Administrator and execute the corresponding commands:

Execution Block Commands:
Block New Outlook (olk.exe):
reg add "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\Image File Execution Options\olk.exe" /v Debugger /t REG_SZ /d "systray.exe" /f

Block Legacy Windows Mail (HxMail.exe):
reg add "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\Image File Execution Options\HxMail.exe" /v Debugger /t REG_SZ /d "systray.exe" /f



Block Legacy Windows Calendar (HxCalendarAppImm.exe):
reg add "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\Image File Execution Options\HxCalendarAppImm.exe" /v Debugger /t REG_SZ /d "systray.exe" /f

Block Classic Desktop Outlook (OUTLOOK.EXE):
reg add "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\Image File Execution Options\OUTLOOK.EXE" /v Debugger /t REG_SZ /d "systray.exe" /f

Reversal / Unblock Commands:
reg delete "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\Image File Execution Options\olk.exe" /f
reg delete "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\Image File Execution Options\HxMail.exe" /f
reg delete "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\Image File Execution Options\HxCalendarAppImm.exe" /f
reg delete "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\Image File Execution Options\OUTLOOK.EXE" /f
Method B: Explorer DisallowRun Policy (Access Denied Notice)
If you prefer end users to receive an explicit administrative warning ("This operation has been cancelled due to restrictions in effect on this computer"), apply the DisallowRun policy via the Registry.
Enforce Disallowed Application List:
reg add "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Policies\Explorer" /v DisallowRun /t REG_DWORD /d 1 /f
reg add "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Policies\Explorer\DisallowRun" /v 1 /t REG_SZ /d "olk.exe" /f
reg add "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Policies\Explorer\DisallowRun" /v 2 /t REG_SZ /d "HxMail.exe" /f
reg add "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Policies\Explorer\DisallowRun" /v 3 /t REG_SZ /d "HxCalendarAppImm.exe" /f

Reversal / Unblock:
reg add "HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Policies\Explorer" /v DisallowRun /t REG_DWORD /d 0 /f


